# 0003. Use Google's module shape: `:core:*` + `:feature:*:{api,impl}`, features see only `:core:domain`

Date: ${SCAFFOLD_DATE}
Status: Accepted

## Context

The project needs a module layout that keeps features independent, builds incrementally, serves several
form factors from shared code, and matches the guidance the team (and Claude's Android skills) read.

References: Android's [modularization patterns](https://developer.android.com/topic/modularization/patterns)
(app, feature, data, common and test modules; one app module per device type), the
[Navigation 3 modularization guide](https://developer.android.com/guide/navigation/navigation-3/modularize)
(each feature split into `api` keys and `impl` content, entries contributed with Hilt `@IntoSet`), and Now in
Android (`:core:*` naming).

Alternatives considered:
- **Flat hybrid** (`:ui :domain :data :testing` + one module per feature, outgoing navigation as callbacks the
  app wires) — fewer modules, but the app must know every navigation edge and Google's material doesn't
  map onto it.
- **Feature-first verticals** (each feature owning presentation + domain + data) — module sprawl in small apps,
  and shared data has no home.

## Decision

Clean-architecture layering: `:core:domain` is the inner layer (business rules and the interfaces they
need), `:core:data` and the UI modules are outer layers that depend on it — never the other way round.

- `:core:model` (JVM) — the app's types: entities and value types (`Photo`, `ThemeMode`). No behavior
  beyond what a value carries, no dependencies.
- `:core:domain` (JVM, `api` on `:core:model`) — the business rules: use cases, repository **interfaces**,
  `DomainError`, `DispatcherProvider`.
- `:core:model` stays separate from `:core:domain` so low-level modules can share the types without
  seeing the rules: `:core:designsystem` themes from `ThemeMode`, `:core:datastore` / `:core:database`
  store entities — none of them can call a use case or implement a repository interface.
- `:core:data` implements the domain's repository interfaces and binds them with Hilt: it depends on
  `:core:domain` (`api` — the interfaces it implements) and on the storage/network modules it builds
  them with (`implementation` — `:core:datastore`, and `:core:database` / `:core:network` once opted
  in). Those modules are tools: each wraps one source (DataStore, Room, Retrofit) and knows nothing of
  repositories (`:core:network` sees `:core:domain` only for the `DomainError` its `catchNetwork`
  returns). `:core:designsystem`, `:core:ui`, `:core:navigation`, `:core:testing` are shared.
- Each feature is `:feature:<name>:api` (Nav 3 keys only) + `:feature:<name>:impl` (internal screens,
  ViewModels, state, strings, an `@IntoSet` entry builder). An impl may depend on other features' `api`.
- **ViewModels call use cases, never repositories** — the domain layer is the presentation layer's only
  entry point, so every rule a screen relies on lives (and is tested) in one place.
- **Stricter than Google:** a feature impl depends on `:core:domain`, never on `:core:data` (or the storage/
  network modules). Only app modules depend on `:core:data`.
- One app module per device type (`:app`, `:wear`, `:tv`, `:automotive`) — see ADR 0009.

## Consequences

- Easier: Google's docs, samples and skills apply directly; features navigate to each other without the app
  changing; every feature has the same shape, testable against fakes of domain interfaces; JVM modules
  build fast and can't touch Android APIs.
- Harder: more modules (two per feature), and a pass-through use case where a screen only reads a
  repository — the price of keeping the domain the single entry point (Google's guide makes the domain
  layer optional; here it is not).
- Reviewer enforces: the dependency rules in docs/architecture.md, `internal` screens/ViewModels/state,
  keys only in `api` modules, `resourcePrefix` on every Android library module (`:app` is exempt).
